<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold IV, Duke of Anhalt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Leopold IV Frederick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold IV Frederick was Duke of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> from 1817 and, after the extinction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> lines, the ruler of the reunited Duchy of Anhalt from 1863 until his death in 1871. His long reign saw the revolution of 1848 in the Anhalt duchies and the start of industrial growth around Dessau. In 1866 he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and joined the North German Confederation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-iv-duke-of-anhalt.jpg" alt="Leopold IV, Duke of Anhalt" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_IV_Anhalt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> on 1 October 1794, the eldest son of Frederick, Hereditary Prince of Anhalt-Dessau, and Landgravine Amalie of Hesse-Homburg, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-landgrave-of-hesse-homburg">Frederick V, Landgrave of Hesse-Homburg</a>. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>. His father died early, in 1814, which made Leopold heir to the duchy, and he succeeded his grandfather Leopold III as duke on 9 August 1817.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 18 April 1818 he married Princess Frederica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a niece of King Frederick William III of Prussia and a half-sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v-of-hanover">George V of Hanover</a>. The match had been arranged by the Prussian court, and the couple had been engaged since 17 May 1816; it expressed Leopold's pro-Prussian policy. They had six children. In 1836 he was a joint founder of the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>, the house order of the Ascanian duchies.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> forced him to grant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> a constitution on 29 October 1848, which he revoked on 4 November 1849 and replaced with a new version in October 1859. Hermann Raster, the son of his trusted official Wilhelm Christian Raster, took part in the revolution and was allowed to choose between prosecution and emigration; he left and became a Republican Party boss in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>On 27 November 1847 Leopold inherited Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> from his distant cousin Duke Henry, and a treaty with Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> in May 1853 joined his lands as Anhalt-Dessau-Köthen. When Duke Alexander Karl died on 19 August 1863, the Bernburg line ended, and on 30 August Leopold took the title Duke of Anhalt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leopold died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> on 22 May 1871 and was succeeded by his son Frederick, who inherited the duchy that Leopold had united. Over his long reign he received many foreign orders, among them the Prussian Black Eagle in 1817, the Russian Order of St. Andrew in 1834 and the Order of the Elephant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1840.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold IV of Anhalt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the extinction of Anhalt-Köthen in 1847 and Anhalt-Bernburg in 1863, Duke <b>Leopold IV Frederick</b> of Anhalt-Dessau ruled all the Anhalt territories.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>Anhalt-Dessau inherited Anhalt-Köthen in 1847 and Anhalt-Bernburg in 1863, after which Duke <b>Leopold IV Friedrich</b> ruled a united Duchy of Anhalt from Dessau.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Dessau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leopold IV, Duke of Anhalt"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5642 5636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5642 5636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
